      "text": "To find the value of \\(\\cos 150^\\circ\\), we can use the angle sum identity for cosine. The angle \\(150^\\circ\\) can be expressed as the sum of \\(180^\\circ\\) and \\(-30^\\circ\\). Therefore, we use the identity:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(180^\\circ + \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\theta = -30^\\circ\\). So we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = -\\cos 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
